1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,800
Picture a high-speed CNC milling center spinning a solid carbide end mill at 15,000 RPM.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,500
Suddenly, microscopic wear on the tool's cutting edge reaches a critical threshold.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,500
Within milliseconds, the tool fractures violently, ruining a twelve-thousand-dollar workpiece,

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:20,500
damaging the spindle, and flinging sharp tungsten shrapnel across the shop floor.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,200
Worldwide, unscheduled tool failure costs manufacturing over $1.2 billion annually

6
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,800
in scrapped precision components and emergency production downtime.

7
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,500
Traditional monitoring systems rely on twenty-thousand-dollar external sensors.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,800
But in real machine shops, these fragile sensors fail within weeks under corrosive coolant.

9
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:44,500
Good morning, respected evaluators and faculty. We are a five-member engineering team

10
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,000
from the Department of Computer Science and Engineering presenting our capstone project:

11
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:54,800
Sensorless CNC Tool Wear Detection in Milling Operations Using Machine Learning and Explainable AI.

12
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:00,500
Our industrial cyber-physical software completely eliminates the need for external sensors.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,500
Here is our team: Priyanka as Lead, along with our engineering members

14
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:11,500
specializing in signal processing, machine learning, software systems, and industrial deployment.

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,500
Rather than bolting fragile hardware onto the machine, our computer science approach

16
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:21,200
taps directly into the CNC controller's internal cyber-physical telemetry.

17
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:26,000
We harvest internal drive currents, motor voltages, and position feedback

18
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:31,000
from the X, Y, and Z linear axes and the main high-speed cutting spindle.

19
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,500
Using the University of Michigan SMART Lab dataset, we stream forty-eight simultaneous channels.

20
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,800
To translate these massive time-series streams into actionable diagnostic features,

21
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,200
our edge digital signal processing pipeline extracts four hundred and twenty-three

22
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:51,500
statistical moments per cutting pass—including mean, RMS, energy, range, and standard deviation.

23
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,800
Crucially, we enforce Bessel's correction with degrees of freedom equal to one

24
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,800
across all sample variance calculations, guaranteeing strict consistency with ISO 3685

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,800
and eliminating small-sample bias across variable-length cutting passes.

26
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,800
Adding process feed rate and clamp pressure yields a structured 425-dimensional feature vector.

27
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,500
Now, let's explore our machine learning architecture and experimental validation rigor.

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,500
In safety-critical manufacturing, black-box deep neural networks are completely unacceptable.

29
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,500
A CNC machinist cannot trust an opaque probability score when an incorrect stop command

30
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:32,000
costs thousands of dollars per hour in interrupted production cycles.

31
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:37,200
We evaluated multiple models and selected an interpretable White-Box Decision Tree.

32
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:42,000
To validate our system, random train-test splitting was strictly rejected

33
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:46,800
because it allows data from the same cut to leak between training and evaluation folds.

34
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:51,500
Instead, we implemented rigorous Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation across eighteen distinct runs.

35
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
On completely unseen CNC trials, our white-box model achieved 77.78% accuracy,

36
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:02,500
an outstanding 90.00% recall on worn tools, and an F1-score of 81.82%.

37
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,800
Forensic error analysis shows that only one worn tool trial was missed due to air-cutting noise,

38
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,800
while nine out of ten dangerous tool wear conditions were intercepted instantly.

39
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
This high recall guarantees maximum protection against catastrophic cutter fractures.

40
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:23,500
Now, let us examine the live software demonstration of our production Streamlit web application.

41
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,500
Here running live in real time is our Streamlit diagnostic dashboard.

42
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,500
First, we upload a raw, unindexed CNC milling experiment CSV file.

43
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,500
The ingestion engine automatically parses twenty-four thousand time-series telemetry samples

44
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:43,200
and retrieves the verified process settings: 6 mm/s feed rate and 4 bar clamping pressure.

45
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:48,500
In our Multi-Axis Telemetry Explorer, operators can inspect live waveform signals.

46
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,800
Switching to the Spindle Current tab, notice the high-frequency harmonic oscillations—

47
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,500
a telltale physical signature that the cutting edge is rubbing rather than cleanly shearing metal.

48
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,800
Under the Position tab, we can observe the micro-trajectory of the Z-axis depth coordinate.

49
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:09,000
Now, we click 'Run Tool Wear Diagnosis'. In under fifty milliseconds,

50
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:13,800
the engine extracts all four hundred and twenty-five features and executes inference.

51
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:19,000
Instantly, a bold red diagnostic alert appears: PREDICTED TOOL CONDITION: WORN.

52
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:24,000
Even more powerful is the Explainable AI Physical Benchmark table below.

53
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:29,000
Our model does not merely output a black-box label; it proves its decision using mechanics:

54
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:34,200
because minimum Z-axis depth exceeded 27.60 mm, upward cutting forces

55
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:39,000
deflected the tool holder upward—proving flank wear beyond the 0.20 mm ISO threshold.

56
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:44,000
The secondary rule verifies cutting trajectory progression via G-code sequence numbers.

57
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:49,500
This transparent symbolic logic allows operators to audit every single wear alert with zero doubt.

58
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,500
To ensure seamless factory deployment, our software compiles into IEC 61131-3 PLC ladder logic

59
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:59,500
and publishes over IEC 62541 OPC-UA, connecting directly to Fanuc and Siemens CNC controllers.

60
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,800
The financial impact is transformative: by eliminating unscheduled machine stoppages,

61
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,800
our system saves a typical CNC machining cell over $117,000 annually,

62
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,500
achieving complete capital payback in just eighteen operational days.

63
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,500
Beyond economic gains, our project delivers critical societal and environmental benefits.

64
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,500
By preventing catastrophic tool shattering, we protect factory operators from flying projectiles.

65
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,500
By optimizing insert lifespan, we reduce premature tungsten carbide scrap by 30%,

66
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:34,500
conserving rare mineral reserves in direct alignment with UN Sustainable Development Goals 9 and 12.

67
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,800
Our complete codebase, validation pipeline, and sixty-page engineering thesis are fully open.

68
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:44,800
We extend our deepest gratitude to our faculty guide and the Department of Computer Science.

69
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:50,500
Thank you for your time, and our five-member team is now ready to defend our project in the viva!
